"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][12]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M12-L1 – Clustering with k-means and hierarchies<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>,\nM10.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Form clusters using distances,\ncalculate the k-means objective and distinguish hierarchical\nmethods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Groups without a known target variable<\/h2>\n<p>A spare parts warehouse wants to group items by consumption and\ndelivery time. There are no predefined categories. Clustering derives a\nstructure from the features; a cluster is therefore initially a\ncomputational group, not a proven natural class. Delivery time in hours\nmust not overshadow consumption in units simply because of a larger\nnumerical scale. Scaling, distance measures and consistent observational\nunits are therefore part of the model. Euclidean distance weights\ndifferences in each coordinate quadratically; for sparse text vectors,\nangular similarity may be more appropriate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>k-means as alternating optimisation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001606_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Werte 0, 2, 8, 10: Zentren 0 und 8 wandern zu 1 und 9; Fehlerquadratsumme 8 auf 4.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Werte 0, 2, 8, 10: Zentren 0 und 8\nwandern zu 1 und 9; Fehlerquadratsumme 8 auf 4.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: k-Means: Zentren aktualisieren.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe seek\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncentres\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand group assignments\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe criterion is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{c_i}\\|_2^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>When the centres are fixed, each point is assigned to its nearest\ncentre. When assignments are fixed, the mean of each group minimises the\nsum of squared distances. Neither step increases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHowever, they do not guarantee a globally optimal solution. Empty groups\nrequire appropriate handling; multiple initialisations reduce dependence\non the starting point.<\/p>\n<p>In the one-dimensional teaching example, measurements are at 0, 2, 8\nand 10. Initial centres 0 and 8 produce groups {0,2} and {8,10}; the new\ncentres are 1 and 9. The sum of squared errors falls from 8 to 4. The\nillustration shows the movement of both centres. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe error would be zero, although each value would stand alone. A\nsmaller objective value is therefore insufficient for choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hierarchical alternatives<\/h2>\n<p>Agglomerative methods start with individual points and repeatedly\nmerge two groups. Single linkage uses the smallest pairwise distance,\ncomplete linkage the largest, and average linkage the mean. For\nEuclidean data, Ward’s method chooses the merge with the smallest\nincrease in the within-group sum of squared errors. In the example, the\nclose pairs 0/2 and 8/10 are merged first; the final single-linkage\nmerge occurs at distance 6. A dendrogram shows the merges and their\nheights. Single linkage can connect seemingly separate groups through a\nchain of intermediate points. In contrast, k-means produces a direct\npartition and works particularly well for compact groups of comparable\ndispersion.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.cluster <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> KMeans<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.cluster.hierarchy <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> linkage<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> KMeans(n_clusters<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, init<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>]]), n_init<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>).fit(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(np.sort(m.cluster_centers_.ravel()), [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(m.inertia_,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(linkage(x, method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;single&#39;<\/span>)[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For new data, the same scaling method should be applied without\nalteration. Also check whether the clusters persist when initial values,\nsamples or measurement conditions change.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the sum of squared errors for just one centre. Solution:\nThe mean is 5; the squares 25, 9, 9 and 25 sum to 68. What practical\ninformation is lost with four singleton groups? The grouping of similar\ncases. Choosing the number of groups therefore requires a combination of\nevaluation measures (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L7<\/a>)\nand the intended use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html\">scikit-learn:\nClustering<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M12-L2 – Density-based clusters and anomalies<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>,\nM4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Trace DBSCAN using\nneighbourhoods and distinguish noise points from confirmed\nanomalies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Density rather than centres<\/h2>\n<p>Measurements along a curved conveyor path may form two elongated\ngroups. A single centre characterises each group poorly. DBSCAN seeks\nconnected regions with sufficiently many neighbours. For a point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_\\varepsilon(x)=\\{y:d(x,y)\\leq\\varepsilon\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A core point has at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoints in this neighbourhood, including itself. A non-core point in the\nneighbourhood of a core point may belong to its cluster as a border\npoint. Points without such a connection remain noise. Connected core\npoints form the backbone of the clusters. A border point reachable from\nseveral clusters may be assigned according to the processing order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A teaching example on the number line<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001548_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Überlappende Radiusbereiche um 0,4 und 0,8; 0 und 1,2 Randpunkte; 3 Rauschen.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Überlappende Radiusbereiche um 0,4 und\n0,8; 0 und 1,2 Randpunkte; 3 Rauschen.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: DBSCAN: Kern, Rand und Rauschen.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The measurements 0, 0.4, 0.8, 1.2 and 3 are analysed with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt 0.4, the values 0, 0.4 and 0.8 are within reach; at 0.8, these are\n0.4, 0.8 and 1.2. Both are core points and are within reach of one\nanother. The values 0 and 1.2 have only two neighbours including\nthemselves, but are border points. At 3, only the point itself remains.\nThe illustration shows the overlapping neighbourhoods and the isolated\nvalue.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.cluster <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> DBSCAN<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>]).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> DBSCAN(eps<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,min_samples<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>).fit(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(m.core_sample_indices_) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal(m.labels_, [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The cluster number is merely an identifier. In this implementation,\n<code>-1<\/code> denotes unassigned noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parameters and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Too small a radius splits even related regions; too large a radius\nmay connect distinct groups. Sorted distances to the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-th\nneighbour, including the point itself, can help guide the choice. An\nelbow is only a heuristic. Different local densities are often difficult\nto capture using a single radius. OPTICS investigates neighbourhood\nstructures across a range of radii; HDBSCAN derives groups from a\ndensity hierarchy. Both are alternatives, not guarantees of unambiguous\ngroups.<\/p>\n<p>Noise points may be measurement errors, but they may also be rare\nvalid operating states. Automatic shutdown would require separate\nvalidation with established error costs. In many dimensions, distances\nalso lose discriminatory power. A different feature representation or a\ndomain-appropriate distance measure may be more important than the\nchoice of algorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What happens in the teaching example with a radius of 0.3 and\nunchanged\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution: No point has another point within the radius; there are no\ncore points and all five values become noise. With radius 0.5 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nall four nearby values are core points; 3 remains noise. The example\nshows why a single setting must not be presented as objective truth.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html\">scikit-learn:\nClustering<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M12-L3 – Principal component analysis and dimensionality reduction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M5.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive PCA from the covariance\nmatrix, determine explained variance and reconstruct observations from\nprojections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Redundant measurements<\/h2>\n<p>Two sensors record different signals that fluctuate strongly\ntogether. A linear combination may describe much of the variation in\nboth quantities. Principal component analysis (PCA) seeks orthogonal\ndirections along which centred data have as much variance as possible.\nThe method requires no target variable. However, high variance does not\nautomatically mean high domain relevance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From covariance to projection<\/h2>\n<p>For a data matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeatures, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the column-centred matrix. The sample covariance is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\frac{X_c^\\top X_c}{n-1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A unit vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces the projection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_cv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>S<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^\\top Sv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubject to the condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^\\top v=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis reaches its maximum at an eigenvector corresponding to the largest\neigenvalue. The remaining directions are chosen orthogonally. For the\nmatrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neigenvectors,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=X_cV_q,\\qquad \\widehat X=ZV_q^\\top+\\mathbf 1\\bar x^\\top.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The proportion of explained variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{j=1}^q\\lambda_j/\\sum_{j=1}^p\\lambda_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the total variance is positive. An SVD of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields the same directions (up to sign and possible choice of basis for\nequal eigenvalues);\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_j=\\sigma_j^2/(n-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A projection by hand and in Python<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001662_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Punkte werden auf die Diagonale projiziert; zwei Projektionsorte und 90 % erklärte Varianz.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Punkte werden auf die Diagonale\nprojiziert; zwei Projektionsorte und 90 % erklärte Varianz.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: PCA: senkrechte Projektion auf die Hauptachse.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The four synthetic points (-2,-1), (-1,-2), (1,2), (2,1) have mean\nzero and covariance matrix<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>10<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>8<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>10<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\frac13\\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;8\\\\8&amp;10\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The eigenvalues are 6 and 2/3. The first axis runs along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit explains 90% of the variance. The point (2,1) receives coordinate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is reconstructed as (1.5; 1.5). Its squared reconstruction error is\n0.5. The illustration shows the perpendicular projection of all four\npoints.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.decomposition <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> PCA<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]],<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> PCA(n_components<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>).fit(X)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p.explained_variance_ratio_, [<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p.inverse_transform(p.transform(X))[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.linalg.eigvalsh(np.cov(X,rowvar<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>))[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>When features are scaled differently, standardisation changes the\nPCA. For later prediction, means, scaling and PCA must be estimated\nexclusively on training data. The number of components can be chosen\naccording to reconstruction quality or validation performance; a fixed\nvariance target alone is not always sufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How much variance is left unaccounted for after projection? Solution:\n10%, or 2/3 in absolute units of sample variance. The sum of squared\nreconstruction errors for the four points is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)\\cdot2/3=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf a small fluctuation happens to indicate a defect, the second\ncomponent may be crucial to quality inspection despite its low\nvariance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/decomposition.html\">scikit-learn:\nDecomposing signals in components<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M12-L4 – Non-linear embeddings and their limitations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L3<\/a>,\nM4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish local from global\ngeometry and evaluate a non-linear embedding using neighbourhood\nmeasures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A curved data structure<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001556_en.svg\"\nalt=\"U-förmige Punktfolge mit lokalem Graphweg zwischen A und B und einer geraden Abkürzung.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">U-förmige Punktfolge mit lokalem Graphweg\nzwischen A und B und einer geraden Abkürzung.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Direkter Abstand und Weg entlang einer Struktur.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Imagine measurement points on a folded ribbon. Nearby points along\nthe ribbon are often similar, even though a straight projection into two\ndimensions may superimpose different sections. Non-linear embeddings\nattempt to condense such relationships differently from linear PCA.\nIsomap constructs a neighbourhood graph and approximates path lengths\nalong the structure using shortest graph paths. The illustration\npresents a U-shaped ribbon with a path along the ribbon and a short\ndirect connection. If the graph is too sparse, it may become\ndisconnected; if it is too dense, shortcuts arise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>t-SNE and local similarity<\/h2>\n<p>t-SNE describes similarities in the original space using\nprobabilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand in the embedding using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor distinct points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\ne j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe symmetrised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare formed from local Gaussian neighbourhoods and normalised over all\nordered pairs. A heavy-tailed distribution is used in the target\nspace:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{ij}=\\frac{(1+\\|z_i-z_j\\|^2)^{-1}}{\\sum_{a\\ne b}(1+\\|z_a-z_b\\|^2)^{-1}},\\qquad C=\\sum_{i\\ne j}p_{ij}\\log\\frac{p_{ij}}{q_{ij}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The minimisation particularly penalises highly similar original\npoints having too little similarity in the target space. Perplexity\ncontrols the effective size of local neighbourhoods. Random\ninitialisation, optimisation and parameters influence point positions.\nThe sizes, distances or orientations of visible islands must therefore\nnot be interpreted directly as feature differences without further\ninvestigation. The classical t-SNE mapping also provides no directly\ndefined transformation for new observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking neighbourhood preservation in practice<\/h2>\n<p>For a U-shaped two-dimensional dataset, we use an additional zero\ncoordinate. The underlying curve is approximately one-dimensional. We\nevaluate Isomap with a small neighbourhood graph and compare local rank\npreservation with an arbitrary ordering. Trustworthiness assesses\nwhether new neighbours in the embedding are actually nearby in the\noriginal space; a high value does not guarantee global distance\npreservation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.manifold <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Isomap, trustworthiness<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linspace(np.pi,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi,<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.column_stack([np.cos(u),np.sin(u),np.zeros_like(u)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> Isomap(n_neighbors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,n_components<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>).fit_transform(X)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> trustworthiness(X,z,n_neighbors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.95<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> trustworthiness(X,np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>).permutation(z),n_neighbors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For empirical data, compare several neighbourhood sizes and seeds,\ninspect the original features and document deviations. If the\nrepresentation produces apparent groups, these should be investigated in\nthe original space or using independent data. A projection is an aid to\ndeveloping hypotheses, not a justification for automatically assigning a\nclass.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Two points are shown far apart by t-SNE. Does this mean their\noriginal distance is greater than that of other pairs of points?\nSolution: No. The criterion prioritises local similarities; global\ndistances are not guaranteed to be preserved monotonically. Check the\noriginal distance under the same preprocessing and the neighbourhood\nranks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/manifold.html\">scikit-learn:\nManifold learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M12-L5 – Association rules and frequent patterns<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M7.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate support, confidence\nand lift, justify Apriori pruning and avoid causal interpretations of\nassociations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transactions rather than feature vectors<\/h2>\n<p>A repair service records the types of material needed for each job. A\ntransaction here is a set, such as {gasket, screw}, without order or\nquantity. Frequent itemsets show recurring combinations. From two\ndisjoint, non-empty sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na rule\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\Rightarrow B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be formed. It describes co-occurrence in the observed collection and\nis not a logical implication.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three measures for a rule<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001510_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Matrix aus sechs Transaktionen DSP, DS, D, SP, P, DS; Support 0,5, Konfidenz 0,75, Lift 1,125.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Matrix aus sechs Transaktionen DSP, DS,\nD, SP, P, DS; Support 0,5, Konfidenz 0,75, Lift 1,125.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Häufige Mengen aus Transaktionen.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransactions and an itemset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(I)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the proportion of transactions containing every item in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor positive denominators,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">conf<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">lift<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{conf}(A\\Rightarrow B)=\\frac{s(A\\cup B)}{s(A)},\\qquad \\operatorname{lift}(A\\Rightarrow B)=\\frac{s(A\\cup B)}{s(A)s(B)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Six jobs contain the following combinations: DSP, DS, D, SP, P, DS (D\n= gasket, S = screw, P = maintenance oil; the original item codes are\nretained). D and S each occur four times; DS occurs three times. The\nsupport of rule D→S is 3/6 = 0.5, confidence 3/4 = 0.75, lift\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75/(4/6)=1.125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration shows the six jobs as a matrix. A confidence of 0.75 is\nonly slightly above the base rate of S. A lift of 1 corresponds to the\nproduct of the empirical individual frequencies; values above 1 indicate\npositive association, not causation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Finding frequent itemsets efficiently<\/h2>\n<p>If an itemset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas insufficient support, no superset can be more frequent, because any\ntransaction containing the superset also contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApriori constructs candidates of size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom frequent sets of size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand discards those whose subsets fail to reach the minimum frequency. At\na minimum support of 1/2, the singleton sets D, S and P and the pair DS\nremain frequent in the teaching example. DP occurs once and SP twice.\nThe triple therefore no longer needs to be checked at this\nthreshold.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> combinations<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>T <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;DSP&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;DS&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;SP&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;DS&#39;<\/span>]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> support(items):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(items) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> t <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> T)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(T)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>frequent <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&#39;&#39;<\/span>.join(c) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> combinations(<span class=\"st\">&#39;DSP&#39;<\/span>,k) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> support(c)<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> frequent <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;P&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;DS&#39;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> support(<span class=\"st\">&#39;DS&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>support(<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(support(<span class=\"st\">&#39;DS&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(support(<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>support(<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.125<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The script counts all small candidates as a reference; it does not\nyet implement Apriori pruning. Eclat, by contrast, uses lists or sets of\ntransaction identifiers and forms intersections. With large datasets,\nthe number of possible rules may nevertheless become very large. Minimum\ncase counts, independent confirmation and domain-informed selection\nreduce chance discoveries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the reverse rule S→D and the rule P→S. Solution: By\ncoincidence, S→D also has confidence 3/4 and the same lift here. P→S has\nsupport 2/6, confidence 2/3 and lift 1; the observed joint frequency\ncorresponds to the product of the individual frequencies. With other\ndata, the confidence of the reverse rule may differ substantially, while\nlift remains symmetric.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://borgelt.net/doc/eclat/eclat.html\">Christian\nBorgelt: Eclat Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M12-L6 – Outlier detection and rare events<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain robust deviation scores\nand Isolation Forest, and assess thresholds in terms of false alarm\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Rarity does not imply a fault<\/h2>\n<p>A machine usually operates at a temperature in a familiar range. A\nrare measurement may indicate a new load condition, a defective sensor\nor damage. Outlier detection seeks unusual cases in a dataset that may\nalready be contaminated. Novelty detection learns from a reference\ncontaining, as far as possible, normal conditions and evaluates new\ncases. Both tasks require a clear context: a temperature of 70 degrees\nmay be normal for another machine model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robust deviation and model scores<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001633_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Grenzen 5,55 und 14,45; sechs Werte im Bereich, Wert 30 außerhalb.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Grenzen 5,55 und 14,45; sechs Werte im\nBereich, Wert 30 außerhalb.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Robuste Ausreißergrenzen mit Median und MAD.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A simple score uses the median\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the median absolute deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">median<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{MAD}=\\operatorname{median}(|x_i-m|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor positive MAD, the normalised magnitude is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1.4826<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(x)=\\frac{|x-m|}{1.4826\\,\\mathrm{MAD}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The factor makes scaled MAD asymptotically comparable to the standard\ndeviation for normally distributed data. However, it does not guarantee\nnormally distributed scores. For measurements 8, 9, 10, 10, 11, 12, 30,\nthe median is 10 and MAD is 1. The value 30 reaches about 13.49, and 12\nabout 1.35. At a threshold of 3, only 30 is flagged. The illustration\nshows the symmetric limits at approximately 5.55 and 14.45. When MAD is\nzero, another justified scale (such as measurement resolution or a\nsuitable quantile) must be used instead of dividing by zero.<\/p>\n<p>Isolation Forest generates random splits in selected features. An\nisolated point is often separated after only a few splits. Comparable\ndeviation scores are obtained across many trees. With several operating\nstates, a local approach such as Local Outlier Factor, which compares\nneighbourhood densities, may be more suitable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A threshold in operation<\/h2>\n<p>Among 10,000 cases with 20 actual faults, a method detects 16 and\ngenerates 100 false alarms. Recall is 16/20 = 0.8, precision 16/116 ≈\n0.138. Despite a high detection rate among the faults, most alarms are\nfalse. In operation, absolute alarm counts, inspection costs,\nconsequences of missed faults and shifts in data over time must\ntherefore be considered. The threshold must be set using development\ndata and later evaluated under realistic conditions.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.ensemble <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> IsolationForest<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">30.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.median(x)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> mad <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.median(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (m,mad)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal(x[<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">1.4826<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>mad)<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ref <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linspace(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">101<\/span>).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> IsolationForest(random_state<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,n_estimators<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>).fit(ref)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># sklearn: kleinere score_samples bedeuten ungewöhnlichere Fälle<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> f.score_samples([[<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> f.score_samples([[<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>]])[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>An algorithm parameter such as <code>contamination<\/code> is not a\nmeasured frequency of actual damage. Manual inspection, the availability\nof independent labels and a strategy for dealing with new normal\nconditions remain necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>If only twelve of the twenty faults are detected but only ten false\nalarms are generated, how do recall and precision change? Solution:\nRecall falls to 0.6; precision rises to 12/22 ≈ 0.545. Which operating\npoint is preferable depends on the consequences. In safety-critical\ndecisions, a deviation score alone is insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/outlier_detection.html\">scikit-learn:\nNovelty and Outlier Detection<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M12-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M12-L7 – Internal and external evaluation of unsupervised methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Unsupervised learning and data mining (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>–<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish silhouette scores\nfrom the adjusted Rand index and plan a robust evaluation protocol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What can be evaluated without class labels?<\/h2>\n<p>When customer groups are unknown, segmentation cannot simply be\nassessed using classification accuracy. Internal measures use data,\ndistances and the resulting grouping. External measures compare against\nindependently known reference groups. Stability analyses check whether\nsimilar groupings arise under different samples or initialisations. Each\nperspective answers a different question; suitability for a practical\npurpose must be established separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Silhouette from two distances<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001700_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Punkt 2 hat Abstand 2 zu Punkt 0 sowie 6 und 8 zur fremden Gruppe; Silhouette 5/7.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Punkt 2 hat Abstand 2 zu Punkt 0 sowie 6\nund 8 zur fremden Gruppe; Silhouette 5/7.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Silhouette: Abstand zur eigenen und zur fremden\nGruppe.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the average distance to the other points in its cluster and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe smallest average distance to another cluster. With a positive\ndenominator,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(i)=\\frac{b(i)-a(i)}{\\max\\{a(i),b(i)\\}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The range is between -1 and 1. For singleton clusters, the score is\nconventionally set to zero; for a valid average silhouette, there must\nbe at least two clusters and fewer clusters than data points. In the\nteaching example from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>,\nfor point 2 the internal distance to 0 is 2, while the average distance\nto 8 and 10 is 7. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.714<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(2)=5/7\\approx0.714<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration shows precisely these three distances. The mean across\nall four points is approximately 0.746. The measure rewards compact,\nseparated groups and may disadvantage elongated or density-based\nshapes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>External pair comparison and stability<\/h2>\n<p>The Rand index compares whether pairs of observations are grouped\ntogether or separated in both the reference and the model. The adjusted\nRand index (ARI) corrects this agreement for chance expectation at fixed\ngroup sizes. Identical partitions receive 1 regardless of cluster\nlabels. Values near zero approximately correspond to the modelled chance\nexpectation; negative values are possible. With reference (0,0,1,1) and\nmodel (0,1,0,1), neither model group shares a pair with a reference\ngroup. At these group sizes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot2/6=2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshared pairs are expected. The ARI is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0-2/3)/(2-2/3)=-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> sklearn.metrics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> silhouette_samples, adjusted_rand_score<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>X <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>]).reshape(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> silhouette_samples(X,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(s,[<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(s.mean(),<span class=\"dv\">47<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">63<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> adjusted_rand_score(y,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(adjusted_rand_score(y,[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]),<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For repeated samples, the ARI can compare stability on observations\npresent in both samples. Document the sampling procedure, scaling,\nparameters, proportion of unassigned points and treatment of noise.\nReference groups may themselves be incomplete or unsuitable for the new\nobjective. If they are used for model selection, the final external\nevaluation requires an independent reference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Two methods have mean silhouette scores of 0.6 and 0.5, but the\nsecond repeatedly identifies a domain-plausible elongated group. Must\nthe first be chosen? Solution: No. Distance assumptions, stability,\nproportion of noise and intended use must also be assessed. In a\nsynthetic test with a known grouping, an external measure can\nadditionally show which structure the method actually recovers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html\">scikit-learn:\nClustering<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
